[table] lossy saline sphere at 64 MHz, 5866 cells, N1curl degree 1 vs 2
  order   cells     DOFs      relL2  power err   |ImP|/ReP   solve s    peak RSS
      1    5866     7591   8.1541%   8.3869%   0.000e+00      0.68    520.4 MiB
      2    5866    39634   0.1405%   0.0058%   0.000e+00      6.48   1187.7 MiB
  (peak RSS is summed ru_maxrss over ranks, not /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.peak — that file is a container-lifetime high-water mark and is not resettable from inside a run; TH-12 step 1's instrument note)

[cost] degree 2 buys 58.0x the field accuracy and 1444x the power accuracy for 5.22x the DOFs, 9.47x the solve wall and 2.28x the peak RSS — sublinear in DOFs on both cost axes here. That is *this* fixture: TH-12 step 2 measured ~20x the wall for 5.42x the DOFs on the coil, at 96.8% of the container memory cap. No production-order claim is made here (TH-12's decision clause is the weekly review's)

[control] degree 1 misses the degree-1 fine-rung record (8.1541% > 3.643% at 17670 cells) while degree 2 beats it on 5866 cells (0.1405%) — 3.01x fewer cells at 25.9x the accuracy
[record] degree 1: relL2 8.1541% vs 8.1541% (drift 4.00e-06), power error 8.3869% vs 8.3870% (drift 1.18e-05) — band 1%
[record] degree 2: relL2 0.1405% vs 0.1405% (drift 5.50e-05), power error 0.0058% vs 0.0058% (drift 1.48e-03) — band 1%
[identity] |Im P|/Re P = 0.000e+00 (degree 1) and 0.000e+00 (degree 2), both under the 1e-09 family bound: the ohmic integrand 1/2 sigma E.conj(E) is real by construction at either order

